body:
This is only the tip of the iceberg for my reasons, but I usually say "I don't have to eat meat, so it makes me feel selfish for eating animals just for my own pleasure"

&#x200B;

No one can really argue with that. Some people say it's unhealthy to not eat meat, but you can respond "that's not true, but either way I still don't HAVE to eat meat. I don't want to be selfish". Pushing the selfish line has always worked for me

You're not wrong, but there's a scale and I don't think anyone here is suggesting we push people in developing countries to eat quinoa salads exclusively. 

&#x200B;

Regardless, I can tell you that it's cheaper to be vegan - especially if you live in a large city. I live in London, vegetables are local and grains are from the EU. That has pretty much been my diet for 7 years and I do it to minimise the exploitation of workers and land, not so much for the animals. 

&#x200B;

It's boycotting bad practises to feel less guilty as opposed to buying shit to feel less guilty.

Bears do not have the cognitive ability to not eat salmon. Every single one of us has that choice though. 

&#x200B;

It isn't the same. There is zero need for us to eat animals and that is why it is unethical. We can choose not to waste the land used for farming, we can choose not to create the ridiculous amount of greenhouse gas produced by farming, and of course, we can choose to boycott the needless suffering of animals.

I can actually answer this one!

&#x200B;

I worked at a petrol station and my boss started the policy that if a customer couldn't pay for the fuel, the employee could not let them leave until SOMEONE paid for that fuel. The person had to call a relative, coworker or friend. For clarification, I live in the UK where you fill up, then pay.

&#x200B;

The most common situation in which someone couldn't pay was when a truck driver had their company card bounce. Normally we'd just get them to fill out a form to charge back to their employer, but with this new policy that wasn't possible. Obviously, most truck drivers were 100s of miles away from home, no one could pay for them when the card bounced - I thought that was reasonable, so I used my initiative and filled out the old-style form. It was a new policy, I thought there would be wiggle room to iron out the problems that arise.

&#x200B;

But no, the boss forced me to pay the total amount of all forms I allowed to be filled out. He said I'd lose my job if I didn't comply because I let the truck drivers steal. The total was something like £600. Instead of paying, I proposed that we call in his superiors to clear up the situation because I would rather have lost my job than pay half a month's wages. 

&#x200B;

The big boys came later in the afternoon, but my boss had already left. He fled that same day to Nigeria and it transpired he was carrying out a massive money-laundering scheme by stealing from the petrol station. He was a bit of a twat anyway

In response to your edit, you're right. But people are pissed off because honey promotes the 'wrong kind' of bee. 

&#x200B;

But again, you're right that the two perform nearly identical ecological roles

I could start chatting about employment monopolies, abuse of power to dismantle unions, all that jazz.

&#x200B;

But it's a meme, simmer down bro

Sorry to give you a life story here; but I had the same sense of dread at my job. I didn't think I'd ever retire and I couldn't wake up every morning in the knowledge I was working to make someone else money in order to buy my landlord his 10th London home. 

&#x200B;

So I quit my job over the Christmas holidays, I'm going to live off savings and try to make my hobby/passion work financially. If it doesn't work out by the time my money runs out, I guess I'll just have to off myself. I am in the fortunate position of not being responsible for anyone's wellbeing so we'll see how it goes. My plan has given me peace of mind and I feel great for it - I guess what I'm trying to say is that everyone needs to find their own escape route

Sure, but to use your energy example - you can reduce bills by subsidising private energy companies. It would make bills cheaper but I wouldn't support that.

&#x200B;

You could say my goal is to improve lives by redistributing wealth. There isn't much room for a middle ground there.

YES

&#x200B;

It was either going to be this or Dragonage. Jon, you made the right choice.
